function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveSet = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  const candidates = collectCandidates(liveCells);\n\n  const result: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const coord of candidates) {\n    const [x, y] = coord.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    const liveNeighborCount = countLiveNeighbors(x, y, liveSet);\n    if (shouldLive(liveSet.has(coord), liveNeighborCount)) {\n      result.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784962706003}],"stderr":"","usage":{"input":14260,"output":4216,"cacheRead":52904,"cacheWrite":0,"cost":0,"contextTokens":14916,"turns":5},"model":"requesty/vertex/claude-sonnet-5@eu","stopReason":"toolUse"}]}}}
{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01PNg5H2tkwgNu2pyhNdjQrP","toolName":"subagent","args":{"agent":"refactor","agentScope":"both","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 10 (all tests in the suite, no more todos remain)\nRecent changes: All remaining spec examples (Rule 3 overpopulation, Rule 4 reproduction, blinker oscillator gen0->1 and gen1->2, block still life, negative-coordinate stability) turned out to already be satisfied by the existing sparse-set Game of Life implementation (NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS, collectCandidates, countLiveNeighbors, shouldLive, nextGeneration) without further code changes -- each was activated as a real (non-todo) test and passed immediately.\n\nThis is the final refactor pass for this kata (test list is complete). Do a final quality pass: check naming, duplication, and simplicity across the whole file now that all behavior is exercised by tests.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\n\nRun autonomously, return when done."},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now let me document the refactoring report.\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- `key`, `countLiveNeighbors`, `collectCandidates`, `shouldLive`, `nextGeneration` — all names clearly describe what each function does, confirmed against all 10 tests (empty input, isolation death, underpopulation, survival, overpopulation, reproduction, blinker oscillation both directions, block stability, negative coordinates).\n- `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS` clearly names the 8-directional offset constant.\n- No function's purpose changed shape now that all rules are exercised — the sparse-set candidate-scanning algorithm was already general enough to handle every rule and edge case without special-casing.\n\nDecision: **Keep all existing names** — they already reveal intent precisely.\n\n**Current Code Mass** (before refactor, `countLiveNeighbors` + `collectCandidates`):\n```ts\nfunction countLiveNeighbors(x, y, liveSet) {\n  let count = 0;                         // binding: count (1)\n  for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {  // loop (5), bindings dx,dy (2)\n    if (liveSet.has(key(x + dx, y + dy))) count++;  // conditional (4), invocations has+key (4), assignment count++ (6)\n  }\n  return count;\n}\nfunction collectCandidates(liveCells) {\n  const candidates = new Set<string>();  // binding (1), invocation new Set (2)\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {      // loop (5), bindings x,y (2)\n    candidates.add(key(x, y));           // invocations add+key (4)\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {  // loop (5), bindings dx,dy (2)\n      candidates.add(key(x + dx, y + dy));      // invocations add+key (4)\n    }\n  }\n  return candidates;\n}\n```\nComponent Count: constants ~0, bindings 9, invocations 12, conditionals 4, loops 15, assignments 6\nTotal Mass ≈ 9 + 24 + 16 + 75 + 6 = **130** (two nested/duplicated offset-iteration loops)\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```ts\nfunction neighborKeysOf(x, y) {\n  return NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.map(([dx, dy]) => key(x + dx, y + dy));  // loop/map (5), bindings dx,dy (2), invocation key (2)\n}\nfunction countLiveNeighbors(x, y, liveSet) {\n  return neighborKeysOf(x, y).filter((neighborKey) => liveSet.has(neighborKey)).length;\n  // invocations: neighborKeysOf, filter, has, .length (4x2=8), loop/filter (5), binding neighborKey (1)\n}\nfunction collectCandidates(liveCells) {\n  const candidates = new Set<string>();  // binding (1), invocation (2)\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {      // loop (5), bindings (2)\n    candidates.add(key(x, y));           // invocations (4)\n    for (const neighborKey of neighborKeysOf(x, y)) {  // loop (5), binding (1), invocation (2)\n      candidates.add(neighborKey);       // invocation (2)\n    }\n  }\n  return candidates;\n}\n```\nComponent Count: bindings 8, invocations ~24, conditionals 0, loops 20\nTotal Mass ≈ 8 + 48 + 20*5(100) ≈ **156**\n\nThe mass calculation shows a sligh