n  return candidates;\n}\n```\nComponent Count: constants ~0, bindings 9, invocations 12, conditionals 4, loops 15, assignments 6\nTotal Mass ≈ 9 + 24 + 16 + 75 + 6 = **130** (two nested/duplicated offset-iteration loops)\n\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n```ts\nfunction neighborKeysOf(x, y) {\n  return NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.map(([dx, dy]) => key(x + dx, y + dy));  // loop/map (5), bindings dx,dy (2), invocation key (2)\n}\nfunction countLiveNeighbors(x, y, liveSet) {\n  return neighborKeysOf(x, y).filter((neighborKey) => liveSet.has(neighborKey)).length;\n  // invocations: neighborKeysOf, filter, has, .length (4x2=8), loop/filter (5), binding neighborKey (1)\n}\nfunction collectCandidates(liveCells) {\n  const candidates = new Set<string>();  // binding (1), invocation (2)\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {      // loop (5), bindings (2)\n    candidates.add(key(x, y));           // invocations (4)\n    for (const neighborKey of neighborKeysOf(x, y)) {  // loop (5), binding (1), invocation (2)\n      candidates.add(neighborKey);       // invocation (2)\n    }\n  }\n  return candidates;\n}\n```\nComponent Count: bindings 8, invocations ~24, conditionals 0, loops 20\nTotal Mass ≈ 8 + 48 + 20*5(100) ≈ **156**\n\nThe mass calculation shows a slight nominal increase (extra `.map`/`.filter` invocations and an extra loop from `neighborKeysOf`), but this reflects **Rule 3 (No Duplication)** winning over raw APP mass: the neighbor-offset iteration pattern (`for (const [dx,dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) key(x+dx, y+dy)`) was duplicated verbatim in both `countLiveNeighbors` and `collectCandidates`. Extracting `neighborKeysOf` removes that duplication — the \"compute all 8 neighbor keys for a coordinate\" knowledge now has a single representation.\n\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Rule 3 (DRY): Extracted `neighborKeysOf(x, y)` to eliminate duplicated neighbor-offset-to-key iteration logic shared by `countLiveNeighbors` and `collectCandidates`.\n- Rule 2 (Clarity): `countLiveNeighbors` now read